          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- *Untrained (placeholder) rows of W1.* Many rows of W1 still hold the identical initial vector (0.020, 0.030, …, 0.530), i.e. pixels that were always 0 in training so their weights never received gradient. Mapping them: row 0 is blank except cols 12–15; row 1 is blank at cols 0–3 and 22–27; row 2 blank at cols 24–27; rows 3–5 blank only in the last 1–2 columns; from row 6 to about row 25 essentially every column (including col 0 and col 27) has trained weights; row 26 blank at the edges; row 27 blank at cols 0–3 and 24–27. So the \"live\" region is almost the whole frame, with only the four corners dead – not a 4‑pixel blank border on all sides.\n- *Hidden units with spatial structure.*\n  - Unit 7 (and partly 9, 10) has a long run of strong positive weights (≈ +0.3 to +0.4) across columns ~4–22 in rows ~22–25: a wide horizontal bar at the bottom of the frame. Units 2, 4, 8, 15 have the same band with the opposite sign (≈ −0.3 to −0.45).\n  - Units 10, 12, 15, 4 vs. 0, 1, 6, 8 have strong, opposite‑signed weights (|w| ≈ 0.3–0.5) at the extreme left columns (cols 2–5) and extreme right columns (cols 21–25) of rows ~5–13 – i.e. two stubs sticking out sideways from the upper body of the image.\n  - Units 1, 8, 9, 12, 13 have broad positive weights (≈ +0.2 to +0.36) over rows 1–3, cols 5–20 (the top centre of the frame) and over the centre body (rows 3–6, cols 8–20).\n  - Units 4, 11, 12 and 15 have strong positive weights, units 0,1,6,10 negative, at rows ~7–13, cols 22–25 (right edge mid‑height), while unit 10 is positive at the mirror position on the left – an asymmetric left/right detector.\n  - Several units respond to a central vertical column (unit 2 positive, unit 3 negative along cols ~12–16 in rows 10–20): an elongated vertical object in the middle.\n- *Output layer.* W2 entries are all of similar magnitude (|w| up to ~0.95), with no single hidden unit dominating one class; b2 small. The bottom‑band detector (unit 7) feeds positively into classes 3, 7, 9, 2 and negatively into 0, 4, 8; its negative‑signed twin (unit 2) feeds negatively into 0,1,2,7,8,9 – i.e. a \"wide low horizontal object\" feature is pushed mostly toward classes 7 and 9 (and 5 via other units). The representation is distributed, typical of a 16‑unit bottleneck on a 10‑class task.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST / KMNIST / EMNIST‑style handwritten characters are size‑normalised into a 20×20 box centred in the 28×28 frame, so rows 0–3, rows 24–27, cols 0–3 and cols 24–27 would all be dead (placeholder) rows. Here col 0 and col 27 are live from row 2–3 onward, row 0 is live in the centre, and row 27 is live from col 4 to 23. That rules out the centred‑character datasets.\n- The dead region is exactly the four corners, and the live shape (wide at top centre, full width in the middle, wide band at the bottom) is what you get from a union of clothing silhouettes: trousers/dresses reaching the top edge in the centre, coats/pullovers/shirts with sleeves reaching both side edges in the upper‑middle rows, bags filling the frame, and shoes forming a long horizontal mass across the bottom.\n- The learned features match those object parts directly: a bottom horizontal \"sole\" band (footwear – sandal, sneaker, ankle boot), left/right \"sleeve\" stubs at mid‑upper height (pullover, coat, shirt, T‑shirt), a top‑centre band (waistband/shoulders/neckline/bag handle), and a central vertical column (trousers/dress). An asymmetric left‑vs‑right sleeve unit is also plausible for distinguishing e.g. ankle boots (asymmetric) from symmetric garments.\n- Grayscale, 28×28, 10 classes, nearly frame‑filling objects with those parts → Fashion‑MNIST. Nothing suggests natural images, digits or letters.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion‑MNIST (Zalando clothing‑article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 75%",
